  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This scholarly book shows early but meaningful uptake: it has 859 recorded downloads, is held by libraries, and appears in open-access discovery systems, indicating real reader access and circulation. Scholarly citation counts are still very limited in the available record, so the impact profile is driven more by usage and discoverability than by a citation trail.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Raymond Hinnebusch\u2019s <em>The international politics of the Middle East</em> has a footprint shaped less by citation accumulation so far than by sustained access and use. The book records 859 downloads and 587 views in the usage data, suggesting that it is being found and read. Its presence in open-access infrastructure, with multiple open instances reported and a gold open-access status, strengthens that reach by making the work readily available to readers beyond a single platform.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Library and holdings signals also point to scholarly circulation. WorldCat and Open Library record holdings, and the subject metadata places the book squarely within international relations, regionalism, foreign relations, postcolonialism, and nation-state studies. That pattern is consistent with a book that is being positioned for research and teaching in political science and area studies, even though the available record does not yet show a large citation base.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The reader-reception data are modest but present. The book has a LibraryThing rating of 7.0, and commercial discovery channels such as Amazon and Goodreads are active in the record, which indicates some broader reader visibility. Those signals should be read as evidence of circulation and engagement rather than as scholarly citation impact.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the evidence suggests a book with solid access, library reach, and ongoing reading interest, while formal scholarly citation uptake remains thin in the current record. This is a profile often seen in books that are still building their citation history or that are used through teaching and consultation as much as through direct citation.</p>",
